(window.LADDER_CARDS["7·logi"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["7·logi"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"7·logi.1.1","q":"A meeting from s to e is going on at t when s ≤ t < e. Why may one ending at 11:00 share a room with one starting at 11:00?","a":"a meeting is not going on at its end, so the two are never going on at the same time","w":["because rooms hold two meetings at once","because 11:00 is not a time","because the first one ends early"],"why":"A room holds one meeting at a time. That is the whole of the rule."},
 {"u":"7·logi.2.1","q":"To find the most meetings going on at once, why check only the starts?","a":"any meetings going on together are all going on at the latest of their starts","w":["because meetings never overlap","because ends are not times","because the first start is always busiest"],"why":"So a day of n meetings has n moments to check: 7c’s finite-search, each case settled by comparing numbers."},
 {"u":"7·logi.3.1","q":"Four meetings go on at once, and there are three rooms. What kind of proof shows they do not fit?","a":"a refutation: suppose they fit, and no-room collides; no rung 7 is used","w":["contradiction proper, with not-not-out","a search of every schedule","a case split by either-way"],"why":"Different meetings going on together need different rooms, and 4 cannot be matched with a part of 3. The statement wanted is a denial."},
 {"u":"7·logi.4.1","q":"First-free-room takes meetings in order of start. Why does it never open more rooms than the most going on at once?","a":"it opens room r only when r − 1 rooms are busy, so r meetings are going on at that start","w":["because it closes a room after each meeting","because it tries every schedule first","because meetings are sorted by length"],"why":"A meeting that finds every room busy proves the rooms were needed. Picking in order of start never strands a meeting."},
 {"u":"7·logi.5.1","q":"There are 3ⁿ ways to give n meetings one of 3 rooms. Why does nobody need to search them?","a":"busiest-start checks n starts and first-free-room writes the schedule in one pass","w":["because 3ⁿ is always small","because the answer is not settled","because either-way picks the schedule"],"why":"The answer is settled by finite-search, so either-way is never needed. But 3³⁰ tries is more than two days at a billion a second."},
 {"u":"7·logi.6.1","q":"Why is “at most 3 at each start, but 4 at some minute between” a move to watch out for?","a":"busiest-start: the most at once is reached at a start","w":["because minutes are not times","because 4 is always reached","because starts are never busy"],"why":"Any four going on together are all going on at the latest of their starts."});
/* end phase F und_r07 */
