(window.LADDER_CARDS["10·logi"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["10·logi"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"10·logi.1.1","q":"What does the item-hours technique say, and what identity does sharing it out give?","a":"if every item arrives and leaves within the window, the hourly counts add to the same total as the stays; shared by the hours, L = λW","w":["the number in the queue is the same every hour; L = λ + W","each item stays one hour; L = λ","the hourly counts add to the number of items; L = W/λ"],"why":"The pairs (item, hour) are counted two ways, by hours and by items. Average number = arrivals per hour times average stay."},
 {"u":"10·logi.2.1","q":"What does the long-run technique say, and why are the bounds M and S not decoration?","a":"with at most M in the queue and stays of at most S, λ_T → λ and W_T → W give L_T → λW; a queue that grows without end has no M, and L_T need not head anywhere","w":["L_T = λW in every window; the bounds are for tidiness","L_T → λ + W; the bounds make the queue empty","with any queue at all, L_T → λW; M and S only speed it up"],"why":"The hours still to come are at most MS, and MS/T → 0. The squeeze, far out, closes the gap between λ_T W_T and L_T."},
 {"u":"10·logi.3.1","q":"What does truck-count say, and what does a half-hour wait at the cooler cost?","a":"a route needs at least trips per day × hours per trip ÷ working hours per truck; the wait on every trip adds 6 × 0.5 = 3 trucks","w":["a route needs one truck for each trip; the wait costs nothing","a route needs trips per day ÷ hours per trip; the wait saves a truck","a route needs hours per trip × working hours; the wait adds half a truck"],"why":"The road is the queue and the trucks its items: trucks on the road = trips per hour × hours per trip. Waiting is part of the stay."},
 {"u":"10·logi.4.1","q":"What does slowest-step say, and what happens when λ > μ_min?","a":"D(n) ≤ μ_min · n: a line passes on no more than its slowest step; at least (λ − μ_min) · n items pile up, a count that passes every bound","w":["D(n) ≤ μ_max · n; the line speeds up to match","D(n) = λ · n always; nothing piles up","D(n) ≤ μ_min · n; the pile settles at λ − μ_min items"],"why":"Every item that leaves has passed the slowest step. Past n = B/(λ − μ_min) the line holds more than B, and every stay grows with it."},
 {"u":"10·logi.5.1","q":"Why is “a faster truck clears the cooler's queue” a move to watch out for?","a":"the cooler is the slowest step and sets the pace; faster trucks only arrive sooner to wait","w":["faster trucks carry less","the cooler works faster for fast trucks","it is sound: speed anywhere shortens the queue"],"why":"Slowest-step: D(n) ≤ μ_min · n, whatever the other steps do."},
 {"u":"10·logi.6.1","q":"Handed a line whose output per hour has settled, how do you find the slow step?","a":"look for the step whose capacity matches the output","w":["look for the step with the most workers","look for the last step","look for the step with the shortest queue before it"],"why":"The line passes on what its slowest step passes on, so the settled output is that step's capacity."});
/* end phase F und_r10 */
