(window.LADDER_CARDS["14·logi"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["14·logi"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"14·logi.1.1","q":"What is the daily cost of an order size, and where is it least?","a":"the ordering cost a day, Kd/Q, plus the holding cost a day, (h/2)Q; it is least at √(2Kd/h), where the two parts are equal","w":["K + hQ; it is least at Q = 0","Kd · Q; it is least at the largest order","the holding cost alone; it is least at one bag"],"why":"Equal-parts from 14h. For the feed store, Q = 500 bags, $2 of each part a day, an order every 25 days."},
 {"u":"14·logi.2.1","q":"How much does an order away from the best order cost, and why does a rough guess do?","a":"the cost is ½(x + 1/x) times the least, with x the order over the best order; near the best order the curve is nearly level, so a rough guess of the costs gives nearly the least cost","w":["the cost grows in proportion to the miss; a rough guess doubles the cost","the cost is the same for every order; no guess matters","the cost is x² times the least; only an exact guess will do"],"why":"Half again too big costs about 8% more; a guess of K twice the truth costs about 6% more. At the best order the slope of the cost is 0."},
 {"u":"14·logi.3.1","q":"What is the reorder point, and why does r = dL run out half the time?","a":"the stock level at which to reorder, what sells in the lead time; sales are not steady, so in about half the cycles the lead time sells more than its average","w":["the order size; it runs out because the order is too small","the day of the month to order; it runs out because the truck is late","the stock at which to stop selling; it never runs out"],"why":"With 20 bags a day and 4 days to deliver, dL = 80. Four-day sales run from about 60 to just over 100."},
 {"u":"14·logi.4.1","q":"How is the stock kept back chosen, and what does it cost?","a":"from the running total of past lead-time sales: choose the reorder point that covered as many past cycles as the store wants; the s bags kept back cost hs every day","w":["by doubling the order size; it costs nothing, since it is sold","by the largest sale ever seen; it costs K a day","by guessing; it costs the price of the bags once"],"why":"Reordering at 93 covers 19 of 20 past cycles with 13 bags kept back, about $0.10 a day. Each extra bag buys less than the last."},
 {"u":"14·logi.5.1","q":"What happens to stock when two stores merge?","a":"the merged store orders √2 times one store's best order and holds 1/√2 of the two stores' stock together, about 29% less for the same sales","w":["the stock doubles","the stock stays the same","the stock halves"],"why":"Double the sales and the best order grows only by √2. The rule has a range, and fails past each edge in a known direction."},
 {"u":"14·logi.6.1","q":"Why is “sales double, so the order doubles” a move to watch out for?","a":"the best order is a square root of the sales, so it grows by √2, not by 2","w":["the best order halves when sales double","the best order does not depend on sales","the best order grows fourfold"],"why":"Q = √(2Kd/h). A big store orders more often for its size, and holds less stock for each bag it sells."});
/* end phase F und_r14 */
