(window.LADDER_CARDS["18·logi"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["18·logi"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"18·logi.1.1","q":"What shape do the bakery's limits cut out, and where are its corners?","a":"each limit keeps one side of a line, so the plans allowed are a bounded convex polygon; its corners are where two edge lines meet: (0, 0), (200, 0), (100, 200), (90, 210) and (0, 240)","w":["a circle of plans about the best one; it has no corners","a triangle with corners (0, 0), (400, 0) and (0, 720)","a convex polygon whose corners are wherever any two of the five lines cross, allowed or not"],"why":"Sides-of-a-line and two-lines-meet (18g). Each corner is checked against the other limits."},
 {"u":"18·logi.2.1","q":"Why is the best plan at a corner, and what does no-middle say?","a":"best-corner puts the largest earnings at a corner, so only five plans need checking: (100, 200) earns $700; no-middle: at a plan with room to spare in every limit, a small enough step in the direction of the earnings breaks no limit and earns more","w":["the best plan is at the centre of the region; no-middle says the middle is always allowed","the best plan uses up every limit; no-middle says a corner is never best","the best plan is found by trying all the plans; no-middle says the region has no inside"],"why":"Lines of equal earnings 3x + 2y = constant slide in the direction (3, 2), and leave the region at a corner."},
 {"u":"18·logi.3.1","q":"What does corner-holds say, and how long does (100, 200) stay best as the round loaf's earnings r change?","a":"a corner is best when stepping from it along either of its two edges earns no more; (100, 200) stays best for 2 ≤ r ≤ 4","w":["a corner is best when it is the highest; it stays best for every r","a corner is best when it uses every limit; it stays best only at r = 3","a corner is best when one of its edges earns more; it stays best for r ≥ 4"],"why":"Along the oven edge the change is −r + 2, and along the flour edge r − 4. At exactly $4 two corners tie, and so does every plan on the edge between them."},
 {"u":"18·logi.4.1","q":"What does worth-at-the-margin say, and what is a spare limit worth?","a":"loosen one limit by one unit, find the new best corner, and the gain in earnings is what that unit is worth, while the same two lines meet there; a limit with room to spare is worth $0 at the margin","w":["each unit of a limit is worth its cost; spare hands are worth their wage","every limit is worth the same; a spare limit is worth the most","the worth of a unit holds however much is added; a spare limit is worth $1"],"why":"One more kilogram of flour is worth $4, one more place in the oven $1, and one more minute of hands $0: 20 minutes were already spare."},
 {"u":"18·logi.5.1","q":"Why is \"bake only the loaf that earns most\" a move to watch out for?","a":"round loaves alone stop at 200, when the flour runs out, for $600; the best mix, 100 round and 200 long, earns $700","w":["the long loaf earns more, so bake only long loaves","baking one kind breaks the oven limit","one kind alone always earns exactly half the best"],"why":"Best-corner: the best plan is a corner of the whole region, where two limits meet."});
/* end phase F und_r18 */
