(window.LADDER_CARDS["22·logi"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["22·logi"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"22·logi.1.1","q":"How many tours do a depot and n stops make, n at least 3, and why is n! halved?","a":"n!/2: each order driven backwards is the same loop, and no order is its own backwards, so the n! orders pair off, two to a tour","w":["n!: every order of the stops is a different tour","n!/2: half the orders do not come home","n!/n: a loop can start at any of its stops"],"why":"Four stops make 12 tours, six make 360, ten make 1 814 400."},
 {"u":"22·logi.2.1","q":"What does one more stop do to the count of tours, and what does a faster machine buy?","a":"it multiplies the tours by the new number of stops, a factor that itself keeps growing; so a faster machine buys only a stop or two","w":["it doubles the tours; so a machine twice as fast handles one more stop every time","it adds n tours; so a faster machine handles many more stops","it multiplies the tours by a fixed factor; so a machine that much faster keeps up"],"why":"Powers of any fixed c lose to factorials (22k)."},
 {"u":"22·logi.3.1","q":"What is the nearest-stop rule, and what are its cost and its fault?","a":"from where you are, drive to the nearest stop not yet visited, and home when none is left; it costs n(n + 1)/2 looks, about half the square of the stops; it leaves the far stops for last and can come home long","w":["try every tour and keep the shortest; it costs n!/2 tours; it has no fault","drive to the nearest stop not yet visited; it costs n looks; it always finds the best tour","drive to the farthest stop first; it costs n(n + 1)/2 looks; it can come home long"],"why":"The courier's nearest-stop tour is 44 blocks against the best of 32."},
 {"u":"22·logi.4.1","q":"What length can no tour beat, and what is such a floor for?","a":"half the sum, over the places, of each place's two shortest links; a floor measures a quick tour against the best without finding the best: 44 against the floor 28.5 is at most about 1.54 times the best","w":["the sum of every place's shortest link; a floor is the best tour itself","half the sum of each place's two shortest links; a floor shows a tour of that length exists","the nearest-stop tour's length; a floor is for finding the best tour by trying"],"why":"A tour uses two links at every place, and each link is counted at both its ends."},
 {"u":"22·logi.5.1","q":"What is out of place in “the floor is 28.5, so a tour of 28.5 exists”?","a":"the floor is under the best tour; it need not be met: here the best tour is 32","w":["the floor is over the best tour, so a shorter one exists","the floor is the nearest-stop tour's length","nothing: a floor is the least tour"],"why":"A bound says what cannot be beaten, not what can be reached."});
/* end phase F und_r22 */
/* phase F und_r28: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["28·logi"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["28·logi"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"28·logi.1.1","q":"What does the middle-store technique say, and why does a far store pull the average but not the middle store?","a":"on one road the total distance is lowest at the middle store, with no more than half the trucks on either side, and the total of squared distances is lowest at the average; squares let a far store pull hard, while distances let it pull no harder than a near one","w":["both totals are lowest at the average; a far store pulls both alike","the total distance is lowest at the average and the squares at the middle store; a far store pulls the middle store","the total distance is lowest at the largest store; far stores do not count"],"why":"Move the northern store 150 km: the average moves, the middle store does not."},
 {"u":"28·logi.2.1","q":"What does the average-point technique say?","a":"the total of squared distances, weighted by trucks, is lowest at the weighted average of the stores, and it grows as W times the square of the distance from there, W being the total of the trucks","w":["the total of distances is lowest at the weighted average","the total of squared distances is lowest at the store with most trucks","the total of squared distances is the same at every point of the region"],"why":"A bowl with its bottom at the average."},
 {"u":"28·logi.3.1","q":"What does the balanced-pulls technique say, and why is a point where the pulls add to zero the best point?","a":"each store pulls the warehouse toward itself with a force equal to its trucks, however far away it is, and the point where the pulls add to zero is the best; the pulls are minus the uphill vector of the total distance G, and G bends up along every line, so a flat point is the one bottom","w":["each store pulls in proportion to its distance; the pulls balance at the average","each store pulls with its trucks over its distance; the balance point is one of many bottoms","each store pulls equally whatever its trucks; the balance point is the middle of the map"],"why":"That is why a far store moves the average more than it moves the best point."},
 {"u":"28·logi.4.1","q":"What does the reweighting technique say, and why does its step never raise the total distance?","a":"re-averaging the stores, each weighted by its trucks over its distance from the present point, is a step downhill; the new point is the bottom of a bowl of weighted squares that sits on or above G and touches it at the present point, so G at the new point is no higher","w":["re-averaging the stores weighted by trucks times distance; it never raises G because far stores get more weight","re-averaging with equal weights; it reaches the best point in one step","stepping along the uphill vector by a fixed size; it never raises G for any size"],"why":"From the average, fifteen reweightings settle to the first decimal."},
 {"u":"28·logi.5.1","q":"What does the heavy-store technique say, and why is the uphill vector not used at a store?","a":"a store is itself the best place exactly when its own trucks are at least the size of the combined pull of all the others on it; at a store G has a sharp point and no uphill vector, so the slopes are tested in each direction","w":["a store is the best place when it has the most trucks; the uphill vector there is 0","a store is the best place when it is nearest the average; the uphill vector there is endless","no store is ever the best place; the uphill vector is used everywhere"],"why":"With 17 trucks or more, South Lake Tahoe would be the answer."},
 {"u":"28·logi.6.1","q":"What does a flat bottom mean for where to build, and what should be reported near one?","a":"near the bottom the total barely changes, so many points are nearly as good; report the region within a stated margin of the best, not one point, and choose inside it by what the total left out: roads, rail, land, workers","w":["the best point must be found to the metre; report its coordinates","any point of the map will do; report nothing","the walk has failed; report the average"],"why":"Why where to build is easier than it looks, and argued over all the same."},
 {"u":"28·logi.7.1","q":"What is wrong with “the best point for distance is the average”?","a":"the average is best for squared distances; for plain distances the best point is where the pulls balance, and a far store moves the average much more than it moves that point","w":["the best point for distance is always a store","the average is best for distance and the middle store for squares","nothing; both totals share one bottom"],"why":"In the Sierra the average is (26.0, 39.8) and the best point about (37.7, 43.4)."});
/* end phase F und_r28 */
